BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Antarctica: The Frozen Desert | Photographs by Vegar Abelsnes
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261104T080000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261104T170000
UID:260914uoO_oxb5KkW0_kBy7CyKyA
DESCRIPTION:1st Street Gallery at WTRSHD will present “Antarctica: The F
 rozen Desert\,” a new body of fine art photography by Vegar Abelsnes\, o
 pening September 26th\, 2026\, and on view September through November.  T
 he exhibition marks a deliberate turn in Abelsnes’ photography\; after m
 ore than two decades of working in a commercial and advertising capacity\,
  he has embarked on a career as a photo and field guide in the polar regio
 ns. The exhibit features work generated over 6 voyages to the Antarctic Pe
 ninsula. He is returning to Antarctica in January and will add the Falklan
 ds and South Georgia to his list of destinations to guide and photograph.\
 n\nTraveling to some of the most remote and inhospitable coastlines on Ear
 th\, Abelsnes has photographed a landscape that exists almost entirely out
 side of human time. The ice in these images began forming millions of year
 s ago\, while Homo sapiens have only existed for roughly 300\,000 years. D
 efined by such a scale\, Antarctica is less a place than a record — vast
 \, indifferent\, treacherous\, and staggeringly beautiful\, holding a kind
  of cosmic truth that Abelsnes says is more important now than ever before
 .
LOCATION:1st Street Gallery @ WTRSHD
URL:https://vegarabelsnes.com/ANTARCTICA:-The-Frozen-Desert/thumbs
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:California Native Creations Art Show
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261104T083000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261104T163000
UID:2609097g-DIhz3hkCPCgRq8t13og
DESCRIPTION:September is the 17th Annual Pomo Honoring Month. On Monday\, 
 Sept. 14\, the 6th California Native Creations Art Show opens Person Senio
 r Wing\, Finley Community Center\, 2060 W. College Ave.\, Santa Rosa\, CA.
  The show is free and open to the public Monday – Friday\, 8:30 a.m.-4:3
 0 p.m.\, through November 19.\n\nThe established and up-and-coming native 
 artists create with digital media\, paint and traditional materials in the
 ir art\, weaving\, regalia\, beadwork\, jewelry\, baskets and quilts. The
  artists include:\n\n·         Meyo Marrufo\, painter\, guest cur
 ator for the de Young Museum and the Sonoma Valley Art Museum\,\n\n·  
        Buffie Campbell\, Vice Chair\, Sherwood Valley Rancheria\, cu
 ltural teacher and master basket maker\,\n\n·         Henry Edwar
 d Frank\, Yurok/Kashaya Pomo\, who will show paintings and work with Legos
  titled Mind Prison\,\n\n·         Digital artist Nicolas \\(Nico
 \\) Vargas\, Kashia Band of Pomo Indians\, whose art was featured at the 5
 5th Annual Stanford Powwow.\n\n·         plus Gloria Carretero\; 
 Rose Hammock\; Rudy Hammock\; Jocelyn Garcia\; Arella Ortega LaRose\, Kash
 ia Band of Pomo Indians\; Angelica Smith\, Manchester Point Arena and Yoka
 yo Rancheria\; Maria Pasalo\; Michelle Napoli\; Martina Morgan\, Kashia Ba
 nd of Pomo Indians\; Hands Lopez\; Shashoni Hanover-Redenbaugh\; Katie Wi
 lliams-Elliott\, Juan Partida\, and Mia Pasalo\, Big Valley Pomo Tribe.
LOCATION:Person Senior Wing\, Finley Community Center
URL:http://www.pomoproject.org/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:S.R. Laws
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261104T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261104T000000
UID:260903FeKDxt0aC0mF7FRAteZk-Q
DESCRIPTION:S.R. LAWS IS OUT THERE: He approaches songwriting with a passi
 on for songs that we all grew up on. His sound rings a familiar bell in yo
 ur head while still delivering ideas completely unique.  He draws inspirat
 ion from outstanding storytellers like John Hiatt\, Lou Reed\, Steve Earle
 \, Neil Finn and Ron Sexsmith. All of their music and others have carved a
  deep groove. \n\nGrowing up out there in Northern California\, he develop
 ed a unique connection to music early on. His father worked as a trash col
 lector\, and he would spend hours sifting through bags of discarded 45 rec
 ords. Each find felt like stumbling upon hidden treasures. Some records we
 re covered in dirt and grime\, while others were pristine. The thrill of d
 iscovering new sounds was exhilarating\, and he could hardly wait to drop 
 the needle and immerse himself in the music.\n\nHe embarked on a pilgrimag
 e to Texas with just two suitcases and $500 in his bank account. His goal 
 was to dive into the vibrant music scene and learn from fellow songwriters
 . Austin\, known as the "Live Music Capital of the World\," offered countl
 ess opportunities for him to grow as an artist. Open mics and songwriters'
  nights became his training ground\, where he soaked up every moment\, lis
 tening to others share their stories through music.\n\nThese days\, he res
 ides in Sonoma County\, California\, working hard as a touring artist and 
 singer-songwriter for his original rock band\, The Heartsleeves. He invite
 s everyone to join him on this musical journey\, sharing his songs one per
 son at a time in this vast and unpredictable world we live in.\n\n“Scott
  Ray Laws is an unique and really cool songwriter…he digs into materials
 \, tells a story in his songs\, and has a clean baritone vocality that is 
 easy on the ears. We had a chance to meet and hang with Scott back in May 
 ..and I HAD to have him back for a Wednesday evening.” Luigi- Owner-The 
 Listening Room.\n\n“..An ORIGINAL songwriter who knows how to tell a sto
 ry clearly in song” -  Scotty O’Neill - Guitarist/Promoter\n\n“ It w
 as such a treat catching your set last night — really refreshing to hear
  something new and original. Thanks for pulling up a chair and hanging out
  with us — we really enjoyed the conversation and the stories behind you
 r songs.”  -Tripp B. - Sonoma Songwriter\n\n“Expect slow burning passi
 on and vivid music …the kind of energy that turns into “let’s stay f
 or just one more!..”  - Doug- Owner/Wine Maker- Spicy Vines\n\n“...His
  lyrics simply tell first-person stories from his own life. Nothing fancy\
 , he's not writing to impress--he pours himself\, as is\, into each phrase
  and melody.”- Rebecca Roudman- Dirty Cello Radio Hour KRCB Radio 104.9 
 FM\n\n“ It is through forthright lyrics that are personal and earnest\, 
 that he composes lyrical pictures. Taking snapshots from the most common w
 alks of life...”  - David Sampson - Producer Studio Secti West\n\n“..r
 elaxed mid-tempo Americana\, capturing in each song the rhythmic pocket La
 ws is known for creating.” -  Coyote Music Review 2024
LOCATION:Café Frida Gallery
URL:https://www.bandsintown.com/e/107812067?app_id=pkvts0on0m&came_from=28
 7&utm_medium=api&utm_source=partner_api&utm_campaign=event
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Maple Magic & Winter Wonder
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261104T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261104T170000
UID:2608249ZT-y1p_7kCeTHp3DubJKg
DESCRIPTION:As the days grow shorter and cooler\, Sonoma Botanical Garden 
 transforms with the beauty of the changing seasons. From the first signs o
 f autumn in October to the brilliant display of rare Asian maples in Nov
 ember and the quiet beauty of early winter in December\, each visit offer
 s something new to discover. \n\nMaple Magic & Winter Wonder invites vis
 itors to experience this special time of year through nature\, creativity\
 , and connection. Throughout the season\, the Garden offers guided walks\,
  hands-on workshops\, lectures\, and special experiences inspired by the c
 hanging landscape. Wander among vibrant fall foliage\, breathe in the swee
 t fragrance of katsura trees\, enjoy creative programs\, and experience th
 e peaceful beauty of the Garden as it transitions into winter.
LOCATION:Sonoma Botanical Garden
URL:https://sonomabg.org/visit/maple-magic-winter-wonder/
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
